 "verdicts": {
  "A study of 33,596 agent-authored GitHub pull requests (arXiv:2601.15195) covering agents such as OpenAI Codex, GitHub Copilot, Devin, Cursor and Claude Code found agents often produce compilable code yet still fail at high rates.": 1,
  "Failed agent PRs generated more comments, revisions and back-and-forth cycles than merged ones, consuming reviewer attention; the paper's recommended mitigations are checking for existing work first, decomposing changes into atomic PRs, and pre-validating against CI.": 1,
  "In the 33,596-PR study, each failed CI check reduced a PR's merge probability by about 15%, and many agent PRs failed on linting, formatting or build misconfiguration rather than logic.": 1,
  "In the 33,596-PR study, merged agent PRs tended to touch fewer files and lines, while failed PRs were larger and sprawling; agents did well on documentation, formatting, CI fixes and small refactors but struggled with corrective logic and feature design.": 1,
  "Over a third of failed agent PRs received no meaningful human interaction before being closed automatically, and a large fraction of rejected PRs duplicated work already resolved, superseded or deferred.": 1
 }
